Say what the root nub is called and what the tree holds

The root nub takes its name from the device tree: InitIOKit() hands the tree
to IOPlatformExpertDevice::init(), which makes a registry entry out of it and
uses that entry as the nub's name.  So the name says whether the tree was
found at all.  If it is not "device-tree", then nothing that matches on that
name is ever offered the nub -- the platform expert that reads the tree
never starts, the nodes under /arm-io are never published, and the storage
controller is never matched.  That is the whole chain, and the name is
where it can be seen from.

The three lookups say the same about the tree itself: whether the branch
the storage driver needs is in it, and whether the interrupt controller is.

The lines are printed next to the one that publishes the personalities,
because that is where the matching starts and because the output there
reaches the screen.  Earlier in the boot there is nowhere to print: the
console is not up yet, and what the kernel writes to the ramoops ring does
not reach the panel.

---

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
@@ -245,6 +245,23 @@
 
 			printf("\nnio: publishing %d static personalities",
 			       (int) personalities->getCount());
+			/* nio: what the tree actually holds, now that it is being
+			 * read for the first time.  The root nub takes its name from
+			 * the tree, so its name says whether the tree was found; the
+			 * three lookups say whether the branch the storage driver
+			 * needs is in it.  All of it lands next to the personality
+			 * line, which is where the matching starts and where the
+			 * output reaches the screen. */
+			{
+				DTEntry found;
+				int hasArmIo = (kSuccess == SecureDTLookupEntry(NULL, "/arm-io", &found));
+				int hasIntc  = (kSuccess == SecureDTLookupEntry(NULL, "/arm-io/intc", &found));
+				int hasUfs   = (kSuccess == SecureDTLookupEntry(NULL, "/arm-io/ufs", &found));
+				printf("\nnio: root nub is named %s",
+				       gRootNub->getName() ? gRootNub->getName() : "(none)");
+				printf("\nnio: tree has /arm-io=%d /arm-io/intc=%d /arm-io/ufs=%d",
+				       hasArmIo, hasIntc, hasUfs);
+			}
 			gIOCatalogue->addDrivers(personalities, false);
 		}
 	}
